(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-cardio-pv-exam-quiz"]={"title":"Cardiovascular & Peripheral Vascular Examination — Set 1 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which structure separates the right ventricle from the left ventricle?","choices":["Chordae tendineae fibers anchoring the papillary muscles","Anterior papillary muscles anchoring the valve leaflets","Interventricular septum","The interatrial septum wall separating the atria"],"answer":2,"exp":"The interventricular septum is the muscular wall separating the right and left ventricles. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is the primary role of the papillary muscles and chordae tendineae together?","choices":["Preventing backflow of blood from the great vessels into the ventricles during ventricular relaxation","Initiating and conducting the electrical impulse that triggers each ventricular contraction","Anchoring the AV (atrioventricular) valve leaflets to prevent them from inverting/leaking during ventricular contraction","Forming the leaflet orifice that regulates flow from the atria into the ventricles during diastole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Papillary muscles anchor via chordae tendineae to prevent AV (atrioventricular) valve leaflets from prolapsing into the atria during systole. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which valve regulates blood flow from the left atrium into the left ventricle?","choices":["Pulmonic valve","Aortic valve","Mitral valve","Tricuspid valve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The mitral valve sits between the left atrium and left ventricle and regulates this flow. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"SA (sinoatrial) node normally generates impulses at what rate?","choices":["40–60 times per minute","100–160 times per minute","20–40 times per minute","60–100 times per minute"],"answer":3,"exp":"The SA (sinoatrial) node is the heart's natural pacemaker, generating impulses at a rate of 60–100 beats per minute. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which node delays the electrical impulse low in the atrial septum before it continues down the bundle of His?","choices":["Bundle of His pathway","SA (sinoatrial) node","AV (atrioventricular) node","Purkinje fiber network"],"answer":2,"exp":"The AV (atrioventricular) node delays the impulse, allowing the ventricles time to fill, before conduction continues down the bundle of His. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"Cardiac output is best defined as:","choices":["The percentage of end-diastolic ventricular volume ejected with each single heartbeat, known as ejection fraction","The volume of blood ejected from each ventricle in one minute, equal to heart rate times stroke volume","The volume of blood ejected from the ventricle with a single heartbeat, known as the stroke volume","The pressure the ventricle must generate to overcome resistance and eject blood, known as afterload"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume, representing the volume ejected per minute. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"The closure of the AV (atrioventricular) valves (mitral and tricuspid) at the start of systole generates which heart sound?","choices":["S4","S2","S1","S3"],"answer":2,"exp":"S1 is generated by closure of the AV (atrioventricular) valves (mitral and tricuspid) marking the start of systole. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"The closure of the semilunar valves (aortic and pulmonic) at the end of ventricular contraction generates which heart sound?","choices":["S2","S4","S1","S3"],"answer":0,"exp":"S2 is generated by closure of the semilunar valves, marking the end of systole and the start of diastole. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"During systole on the left side of the heart, which valve is open and which is closed?","choices":["Aortic valve open; mitral valve closed","Both aortic and mitral valves open","Both aortic and mitral valves closed","Aortic valve closed; mitral valve open"],"answer":0,"exp":"During systole the aortic valve opens to allow ejection into the aorta while the mitral valve closes to prevent regurgitation into the left atrium. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"Pulse pressure is defined as:","choices":["The peak arterial pressure generated during ventricular systole","The pressure drop measured across a stenotic aortic valve","The difference between systolic and diastolic pressure","The mean arterial pressure averaged across the cardiac cycle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pulse pressure is the difference between the highest (systolic) and lowest (diastolic) arterial pressures. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"For a blood pressure of 120/80 mm Hg, what is the pulse pressure, and is it within the normal range?","choices":["80 mm Hg, which is abnormally high for this reading","40 mm Hg, which is within the normal 30–40 mm Hg range","120 mm Hg, which is abnormally elevated for this reading","20 mm Hg, which is abnormally low for this patient"],"answer":1,"exp":"120 − 80 = 40 mm Hg, which falls within the normal pulse pressure range of 30–40 mm Hg. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"On the Littmann Cardiology IV stethoscope, the bell side (light pressure) is best used for which findings?","choices":["Only carotid bruits","Normal heartbeats, breath sounds, and bowel sounds","Only pediatric heart sounds","Heart murmurs, S3/S4 heart sounds, and bruits"],"answer":3,"exp":"The bell, used with light pressure, best transmits low-frequency sounds such as murmurs, S3/S4, and bruits. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a concerning cardiac symptom to ask about during history taking?","choices":["Myalgia (muscle ache)","Eye light sensitivity","Tinnitus (ear ringing)","Syncope (fainting)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Syncope is listed among the concerning cardiac symptoms to elicit in the history. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which social history element is specifically relevant to concerning cardiac history?","choices":["Recent travel history","Smoking or smoke exposure","Dietary preference for vegetarianism","Occupation as a teacher"],"answer":1,"exp":"Smoking/smoke exposure is specifically listed as a relevant social history element for cardiac risk. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 19"},{"q":"For the cardiac examination, how should the patient be positioned?","choices":["Sitting fully upright at 90 degrees","Supine with the head elevated 30 degrees","Flat supine with zero head elevation","Prone with the head turned laterally"],"answer":1,"exp":"The patient is placed supine with the head of the bed elevated 30 degrees for the cardiac exam. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"If you are right-handed, where should you stand for best access to the heart during the cardiac exam?","choices":["At the foot of the bed","On the patient's right side","On the patient's left side","Behind the patient's head"],"answer":1,"exp":"A right-handed examiner should stand on the patient's right side for best access. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"When auscultating for carotid bruits, why must you ask the patient to stop breathing for about 10 seconds?","choices":["To allow simultaneous bimanual palpation of both carotid pulses","To transiently lower the patient's blood pressure before listening","To transiently increase blood flow velocity through the carotid","To eliminate breath sounds that could obscure a subtle bruit"],"answer":3,"exp":"Holding breath removes tracheal/breath sound interference so a faint carotid bruit can be heard clearly. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Where should the stethoscope be placed to auscultate for carotid bruits?","choices":["Directly over the sternal notch at the base of the neck","Near the upper end of the thyroid cartilage, below the angle of the jaw","Over the midclavicular line at the fifth intercostal space","At the angle of the mandible only, never below that level"],"answer":1,"exp":"This location overlies the carotid bifurcation and reduces confusion with a transmitted cardiac murmur. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which layer of an artery is where atherosclerosis characteristically forms?","choices":["Vasa vasorum","Adventitia","Intima","Media"],"answer":2,"exp":"The intima, the single continuous endothelial lining, is where atherosclerosis characteristically forms. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which arterial layer consists of smooth muscle with elastic properties that accommodate blood pressure and flow?","choices":["Media","Adventitia","Endothelium alone","Intima"],"answer":0,"exp":"The media contains smooth muscle with elastin, accommodating pressure and flow changes. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"Arterioles are best described as:","choices":["Thin-walled exchange vessels with only an endothelial lining, lacking smooth muscle in their walls","Thin-walled channels that transport lymph fluid rather than blood back toward the venous system","Large, highly elastic conduit vessels, like the aorta, that buffer pulsatile arterial pressure","Resistance vessels whose smooth muscle tone significantly determines systemic vascular resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Arterioles are resistance vessels, and their smooth muscle tone is a major determinant of systemic vascular resistance. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which of the following should be asked about when taking a peripheral vascular history?","choices":["Bowel habits, stool consistency, and abdominal symptoms","Visual acuity, eye pain, and history of eye trauma","Skin color, temperature, and symptoms provoked by exertion","Hearing loss, tinnitus, and history of ear infections"],"answer":2,"exp":"These are specifically listed elements to ask about when assessing peripheral vascular history. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 38"},{"q":"During inspection of the lower extremities, what must be ensured regarding patient positioning and privacy?","choices":["No draping is required as long as the exam room door remains closed","The patient is supine and appropriately draped, with external genitalia covered","The patient remains fully clothed throughout the entire inspection","The patient stands completely unclothed for optimal visualization"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lower extremity inspection requires supine positioning with appropriate draping and coverage of the external genitalia. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"When inspecting the extremities, which features should be noted?","choices":["Only the presence or absence of a gross deficit in manual muscle strength testing, with nothing else recorded","Only whether a palpable pulse can be felt at each of the standard peripheral pulse points","Only the patient's self-reported pain intensity rated on a numeric 1 to 10 pain scale","Size, symmetry, swelling, venous pattern, skin color/texture, temperature, hair growth, ulcerations, and nail beds"],"answer":3,"exp":"All of these features should be systematically noted during extremity inspection. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"Where should the radial pulse be palpated?","choices":["Behind and below the medial malleolus, using the finger pads against the tibia","On the extensor surface of the dorsal wrist, just distal to the radius","Just medial to the biceps tendon at the antecubital crease, with the elbow slightly flexed","With the finger pads on the flexor surface of the lateral wrist, with the wrist partially flexed"],"answer":3,"exp":"The radial pulse is palpated with finger pads on the flexor surface of the lateral wrist; partial wrist flexion may aid palpation. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"Where is the brachial pulse palpated?","choices":["On the flexor surface of the lateral wrist, with the wrist partially flexed","Just medial to the biceps tendon at the antecubital crease, with the elbow slightly flexed","Midway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic symphysis, below the inguinal ligament","Lateral to the extensor tendon of the great toe, on the dorsum of the foot"],"answer":1,"exp":"The brachial pulse is felt just medial to the biceps tendon at the antecubital crease with the elbow slightly flexed. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"Epitrochlear lymph nodes are palpated in which location?","choices":["Along the anterior wrist crease, just proximal to the thenar eminence, with the wrist held in slight extension","In the groove between the biceps and triceps muscles, about 3 cm above the medial epicondyle, with the elbow flexed ~90 degrees","Directly over the antecubital crease, medial to the biceps tendon, with the elbow fully extended and supinated","In the axilla with the arm fully abducted, pressing the fingertips up and in toward the chest wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"This is the correct technique and location for palpating epitrochlear nodes. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"Are epitrochlear nodes typically palpable in healthy individuals?","choices":["Yes, but only in pediatric patients","Only palpable in patients with high BMI (body mass index)","No, they are usually not palpable in healthy individuals","Yes, they are always palpable and up to 3 cm is normal"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture states epitrochlear nodes are usually not palpable in healthy people. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"Pulsus parvus, referring to weak pulses, is classically seen in which condition?","choices":["Severe, longstanding aortic regurgitation","Normal healthy aging, without disease","Atherosclerotic peripheral vascular disease","Anxiety-induced sinus tachycardia alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pulsus parvus (weak pulses) is classically associated with atherosclerotic PVD (peripheral vascular disease). — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"Pulsus tardus, referring to sluggish pulses, typically occurs in which settings?","choices":["Fever with a hyperdynamic circulation","Hyperthyroidism with a hyperkinetic pulse","Aortic stenosis or low cardiac output","Isolated dehydration without heart disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pulsus tardus (a slow-rising, sluggish pulse) is classically seen with aortic stenosis or states of low cardiac output. — Cardiovascular and Peripheral Vascular Examination lecture, Slide 34"}]};
